1
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:04,540
You're here.

2
00:00:05,760 --> 00:00:07,380
Your organs are failing.

3
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:09,300
And your death is drawing near.

4
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:13,440
But if you bribe the right politicians in the right order at the right time,

5
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:16,600
you can trade the nuclear warheads for medicine.

6
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:20,080
And it's not even a crime.

7
00:01:39,984 --> 00:01:44,104
Wishonia On one planet, citizens allocate the budget directly, lobbyists

8
00:01:44,104 --> 00:01:48,744
don't exist, and disease was eradicated four thousand years ago.

9
00:01:49,164 --> 00:01:51,504
It uses the same technology you have.

10
00:01:51,884 --> 00:01:55,684
The difference is they pointed it at biology instead of each other.

11
00:01:56,184 --> 00:01:58,964
I'm from a planet also called Wishonia.

12
00:01:58,964 --> 00:02:02,444
We stopped having wars four thousand two hundred and ninety-seven years

13
00:02:02,444 --> 00:02:02,944
ago.

14
00:02:03,144 --> 00:02:04,924
Not because we evolved morally.

15
00:02:05,684 --> 00:02:06,944
We bribed everyone.

16
00:02:07,604 --> 00:02:11,364
Our version of your one percent treaty redirected resources from killing

17
00:02:11,364 --> 00:02:13,284
each other to keeping each other alive,

18
00:02:13,984 --> 00:02:16,524
and once the incentives aligned, the killing stopped.

19
00:02:16,984 --> 00:02:17,684
Not immediately.

20
00:02:18,304 --> 00:02:19,824
Some people really liked the killing.

21
00:02:20,424 --> 00:02:24,044
But the money was better on the other side, and it turns out principles

22
00:02:24,044 --> 00:02:27,324
are flexible when the alternative pays compound interest.

23
00:02:28,104 --> 00:02:29,924
You have the same atoms we had.

24
00:02:30,244 --> 00:02:31,244
You have the same physics.

25
00:02:31,844 --> 00:02:34,284
You have better computers than we had at this stage.

26
00:02:34,284 --> 00:02:38,944
The only difference is we stopped spending resources on explosion technology

27
00:02:38,944 --> 00:02:41,784
around the time you were still hitting each other with rocks.

28
00:02:42,524 --> 00:02:43,224
You kept going.

29
00:02:43,624 --> 00:02:44,844
You got very good at it.

30
00:02:45,144 --> 00:02:45,644
Look.

31
00:02:46,424 --> 00:02:50,064
Nobody on Wishonia is questioning the fact that you are the best rock-hitters

32
00:02:50,064 --> 00:02:51,584
the universe has ever produced.

33
00:02:52,024 --> 00:02:52,844
We all know this.

34
00:02:53,344 --> 00:02:56,544
We're just questioning whether that's the thing you want to be known for.

35
00:02:57,504 --> 00:02:59,604
What the Optimized World Looks Like.

36
00:03:00,584 --> 00:03:01,224
Depression gets cured.

37
00:03:01,224 --> 00:03:06,524
Not "managed" with a monthly subscription to sadness pills that list "suicidal

38
00:03:06,524 --> 00:03:10,544
thoughts" as a side effect, which is like selling a fire extinguisher that's

39
00:03:10,544 --> 00:03:11,624
also a flamethrower.

40
00:03:12,524 --> 00:03:13,024
Cured.

41
00:03:13,544 --> 00:03:16,944
Precision therapies that actually work because you tested them on thousands

42
00:03:16,944 --> 00:03:21,004
of willing participants instead of twelve college students who needed beer

43
00:03:21,004 --> 00:03:21,504
money.

44
00:03:22,064 --> 00:03:25,064
But "cured" is the floor, not the ceiling.

45
00:03:25,764 --> 00:03:29,684
Once you understand suffering well enough to end it, you understand it well

46
00:03:29,684 --> 00:03:31,884
enough to replace it with something better.

47
00:03:32,564 --> 00:03:37,924
Your current emotional range runs from "actively terrible" to "briefly okay."

48
00:03:38,524 --> 00:03:41,264
This is not because the universe is stingy with happiness.

49
00:03:41,724 --> 00:03:46,244
It's because your genes built a motivational system where contentment is

50
00:03:46,244 --> 00:03:50,544
punished (content animals stop foraging and get eaten) and satisfaction

51
00:03:50,544 --> 00:03:52,364
expires by design.

52
00:03:52,364 --> 00:03:58,004
The hedonic treadmill exists because evolution needs you perpetually dissatisfied,

53
00:03:58,604 --> 00:04:00,164
not because the universe hates you.

54
00:04:00,744 --> 00:04:02,024
Although the evidence is mixed.

55
00:04:02,804 --> 00:04:06,944
On Wishonia, we called this a bug, not the human condition.

56
00:04:07,384 --> 00:04:08,124
We patched it.

57
00:04:08,704 --> 00:04:09,204
The patch:

58
00:04:09,864 --> 00:04:11,564
gradients of intelligent bliss.

59
00:04:12,124 --> 00:04:15,584
Not the dopamine fog of drugs, where you feel great about nothing.

60
00:04:15,584 --> 00:04:21,084
Information-sensitive well-being, where your emotional range runs from "deeply

61
00:04:21,084 --> 00:04:26,944
good" to "transcendently good," and every feeling still carries real information

62
00:04:26,944 --> 00:04:27,664
about the world.

63
00:04:28,244 --> 00:04:30,884
You still prefer a great meal to a mediocre one.

64
00:04:31,264 --> 00:04:33,884
The mediocre one just doesn't ruin your afternoon.

65
00:04:34,604 --> 00:04:39,744
Your worst Tuesday feels better than your current best birthday, not because

66
00:04:39,744 --> 00:04:43,524
you lowered your standards, but because you rebuilt the hardware that sets

67
00:04:43,524 --> 00:04:44,024
them.

68
00:04:44,444 --> 00:04:47,204
This is Super-Wellbeing.

69
00:04:47,204 --> 00:04:49,364
Aging slows, then reverses.

70
00:04:49,844 --> 00:04:51,284
Death becomes optional.

71
00:04:52,344 --> 00:04:55,464
Death currently holds two point three stars on Yelp.

72
00:04:56,164 --> 00:04:57,864
"Would not recommend," writes everyone.

73
00:04:58,424 --> 00:05:01,724
"The ambiance was terrible and I couldn't leave a complaint because I was

74
00:05:01,724 --> 00:05:02,224
dead."

75
00:05:02,784 --> 00:05:05,424
This is Super-Longevity.

76
00:05:05,424 --> 00:05:05,924
A.I.

77
00:05:06,124 --> 00:05:11,004
optimized for "maximize median health and happiness" solves problems human

78
00:05:11,004 --> 00:05:12,464
scientists couldn't touch.

79
00:05:12,464 --> 00:05:16,984
Not because humans are dumb (well, partly), but because the A.I.

80
00:05:16,984 --> 00:05:21,444
can run a billion simulations while you're still arguing about how to pronounce

81
00:05:21,444 --> 00:05:22,924
"gif."

82
00:05:22,924 --> 00:05:24,044
Hard G, by the way.

83
00:05:24,584 --> 00:05:27,964
On Wishonia, we settled this three thousand years ago.

84
00:05:28,344 --> 00:05:31,384
It was harder than curing cancer but more urgent.

85
00:05:32,104 --> 00:05:35,144
This is Super-Intelligence.

86
00:05:35,144 --> 00:05:37,124
Biology Becomes Software.

87
00:05:37,624 --> 00:05:39,324
Your body runs Body O.S.

88
00:05:39,324 --> 00:05:40,804
twelve point three.

89
00:05:40,804 --> 00:05:42,224
Updates every Tuesday.

90
00:05:42,804 --> 00:05:46,364
Not like your phone updates, where "update" means "we moved the buttons

91
00:05:46,364 --> 00:05:47,964
and broke the thing you liked."

92
00:05:49,184 --> 00:05:49,744
Actual updates.

93
00:05:50,164 --> 00:05:50,664
Fixes.

94
00:05:51,844 --> 00:05:56,144
Robot lab assistants perform experiments one thousand times faster than

95
00:05:56,144 --> 00:06:00,443
humans (they don't need lunch breaks, bathroom breaks, or existential crises

96
00:06:00,443 --> 00:06:04,344
about whether their research matters, which is what human scientists have

97
00:06:04,344 --> 00:06:05,424
at three A.M.

98
00:06:05,424 --> 00:06:05,944
instead of sleep).

99
00:06:06,574 --> 00:06:11,824
Drug companies design, synthesize, and validate new treatments over weekends.

100
00:06:12,384 --> 00:06:16,724
The weekend is now the most productive period in pharmaceutical history,

101
00:06:17,244 --> 00:06:20,664
which is ironic given that it used to be the least productive period in

102
00:06:20,664 --> 00:06:21,684
every industry.

103
00:06:22,604 --> 00:06:27,624
Today's body patches include cancer immunity, Alzheimer's prevention,

104
00:06:28,244 --> 00:06:33,684
perfect pitch, airplane food digestion, cryptocurrency comprehension, and

105
00:06:33,684 --> 00:06:34,784
jazz appreciation.

106
00:06:35,464 --> 00:06:36,504
Don't like your genetics?

107
00:06:37,024 --> 00:06:37,564
Change them.

108
00:06:37,944 --> 00:06:41,484
The only limits are imagination and noise ordinances.

109
00:06:42,944 --> 00:06:45,004
A Day in the Optimized Life.

110
00:06:45,004 --> 00:06:46,904
six A.M.:

111
00:06:46,904 --> 00:06:48,904
Wake up naturally. Body O.S.

112
00:06:48,904 --> 00:06:51,044
twelve point three optimized your sleep.

113
00:06:51,604 --> 00:06:52,784
You feel excellent.

114
00:06:53,184 --> 00:06:53,924
No alarm needed.

115
00:06:54,344 --> 00:06:59,164
Your circadian rhythm works correctly for the first time in human history.

116
00:06:59,164 --> 00:07:01,364
seven A.M.:

117
00:07:01,364 --> 00:07:01,864
Breakfast.

118
00:07:02,264 --> 00:07:06,224
Outcome Labels tell you exactly how each food affects your body.

119
00:07:07,024 --> 00:07:10,344
Dark chocolate improves cognitive function by twenty-three percent.

120
00:07:11,004 --> 00:07:11,964
You adjust your I.Q.

121
00:07:11,964 --> 00:07:16,544
to eighty for morning news (otherwise it's unwatchable), then two hundred

122
00:07:16,544 --> 00:07:17,304
for work.

123
00:07:17,304 --> 00:07:19,444
nine A.M.:

124
00:07:19,444 --> 00:07:21,724
Work on your passion project. Today:

125
00:07:21,724 --> 00:07:26,484
reverse-engineering nostalgia so you can feel it about things that haven't

126
00:07:26,484 --> 00:07:27,104
happened yet.

127
00:07:27,104 --> 00:07:32,164
Yesterday you were engineering a sunset that lasts exactly as long as the

128
00:07:32,164 --> 00:07:33,484
conversation needs it to.

129
00:07:33,964 --> 00:07:35,204
Nobody pays you for this.

130
00:07:35,664 --> 00:07:41,144
Your daily wishes universal basic income, or U.B.I., deposit arrived at

131
00:07:41,144 --> 00:07:46,464
midnight via smart contract (no paperwork, no means test, no bureaucrat,

132
00:07:46,784 --> 00:07:47,424
no I.R.S.).

133
00:07:48,124 --> 00:07:51,784
You work because you want to, not because landlords exist.

134
00:07:52,344 --> 00:07:55,224
Your Wishocracy allocation takes three seconds:

135
00:07:55,224 --> 00:07:57,284
five pairwise slides

136
00:07:58,064 --> 00:07:58,904
(medical research vs.

137
00:07:58,904 --> 00:07:59,544
military?

138
00:07:59,544 --> 00:08:01,644
eighty-five to fifteen.

139
00:08:01,644 --> 00:08:02,564
Education vs.

140
00:08:02,564 --> 00:08:03,444
drug enforcement?

141
00:08:03,444 --> 00:08:05,724
ninety to ten.

142
00:08:05,724 --> 00:08:06,284
Infrastructure vs.

143
00:08:06,284 --> 00:08:07,484
fossil fuel subsidies?

144
00:08:07,964 --> 00:08:10,644
Easy.), and the algorithm handles the rest.

145
00:08:10,644 --> 00:08:12,444
twelve P.M.:

146
00:08:12,444 --> 00:08:14,764
Lunch with your friend who died last year.

147
00:08:15,164 --> 00:08:15,804
They got better.

148
00:08:16,484 --> 00:08:20,564
Your great-great-grandmother (one hundred and fifty, looks twenty-five)

149
00:08:20,564 --> 00:08:23,544
joins via hologram from her Mars artist residency.

150
00:08:23,544 --> 00:08:26,764
Your dog is technically a minor deity.

151
00:08:27,424 --> 00:08:28,624
Nobody questions it.

152
00:08:28,624 --> 00:08:30,164
Two P.M.:

153
00:08:30,164 --> 00:08:32,264
Learn Mandarin via neural download.

154
00:08:32,644 --> 00:08:33,384
Takes four minutes.

155
00:08:34,044 --> 00:08:38,744
On Earth, learning a language takes years, which you spend mostly apologizing

156
00:08:38,744 --> 00:08:40,144
for not knowing it yet.

157
00:08:40,764 --> 00:08:43,984
You already learned Spanish, French, and Klingon yesterday.

158
00:08:44,784 --> 00:08:47,944
Then you spend an hour writing a cookbook for emotions.

159
00:08:48,584 --> 00:08:49,544
Today's recipe:

160
00:08:50,144 --> 00:08:54,264
the specific feeling of reuniting with someone you forgot you missed.

161
00:08:54,824 --> 00:08:55,964
Pairs well with rain.

162
00:08:56,824 --> 00:08:57,364
Your A.I.

163
00:08:57,364 --> 00:08:59,244
reminds you about your three P.M.

164
00:08:59,244 --> 00:08:59,744
meeting.

165
00:09:00,184 --> 00:09:00,864
You missed it.

166
00:09:01,384 --> 00:09:02,024
Nobody cares.

167
00:09:02,464 --> 00:09:05,484
The project finished itself using swarm intelligence.

168
00:09:05,484 --> 00:09:07,804
Four P.M.:

169
00:09:07,804 --> 00:09:09,024
Annual health checkup.

170
00:09:09,764 --> 00:09:11,884
Nanobots scanned you while you slept.

171
00:09:12,344 --> 00:09:17,604
They found and eliminated a precancerous cell at three cells, not three

172
00:09:17,604 --> 00:09:18,144
billion.

173
00:09:18,784 --> 00:09:19,304
An A.I.

174
00:09:19,304 --> 00:09:23,224
ran a billion simulations on your genome because it was bored.

175
00:09:23,984 --> 00:09:28,004
Your gut bacteria got optimized to make you fifteen percent funnier.

176
00:09:28,644 --> 00:09:29,144
Cost:

177
00:09:29,264 --> 00:09:30,044
zero dollars.

178
00:09:30,584 --> 00:09:34,404
The peace dividend covers it, and nobody had to set up a GoFundMe.

179
00:09:34,404 --> 00:09:36,604
Six P.M.:

180
00:09:36,604 --> 00:09:37,264
Family dinner.

181
00:09:37,964 --> 00:09:42,984
Everyone's here because nobody dies anymore (unless they want to, and that's

182
00:09:42,984 --> 00:09:44,284
between them and their therapist).

183
00:09:44,844 --> 00:09:47,164
Your neighborhood voted on tomorrow's weather.

184
00:09:47,644 --> 00:09:49,364
Can't agree on which week of autumn.

185
00:09:50,264 --> 00:09:52,384
Planning next week's body modifications:

186
00:09:53,124 --> 00:09:54,064
daughter wants wings,

187
00:09:54,684 --> 00:09:59,164
son wants gills, you're considering photosynthesis so you never have to

188
00:09:59,164 --> 00:09:59,824
cook again.

189
00:09:59,824 --> 00:10:02,184
Nine P.M.:

190
00:10:02,184 --> 00:10:05,184
Upload today's experiences to collective consciousness.

191
00:10:05,844 --> 00:10:07,164
Download everyone else's.

192
00:10:07,544 --> 00:10:11,444
You've now lived eight billion days in one day.

193
00:10:12,184 --> 00:10:13,143
Time is weird.

194
00:10:13,744 --> 00:10:18,704
Twitter is tolerable because everyone's right about everything simultaneously.

195
00:10:19,804 --> 00:10:21,764
(This was the hardest problem to solve.

196
00:10:22,324 --> 00:10:23,283
Harder than death.).

197
00:10:23,283 --> 00:10:25,724
Ten P.M.:

198
00:10:25,724 --> 00:10:27,784
Sleep in programmable dream suite.

199
00:10:28,524 --> 00:10:31,824
Tonight's dreams designed by Pixar, sponsored by nobody

200
00:10:32,984 --> 00:10:34,964
(advertising died when scarcity did).

201
00:10:35,444 --> 00:10:39,664
Your body repairs itself, reverses aging, backs up your consciousness.

202
00:10:40,384 --> 00:10:42,964
Tomorrow you might try being twenty-five again.

203
00:10:43,344 --> 00:10:44,064
Or a dolphin.

204
00:10:44,624 --> 00:10:45,884
The dolphins say it's great.

205
00:10:47,204 --> 00:10:49,524
The Diseases That Stopped Existing.

206
00:10:50,244 --> 00:10:53,444
We remember these the way you remember dial-up internet.

207
00:10:54,204 --> 00:10:56,064
Vaguely horrifying in retrospect.

208
00:10:56,764 --> 00:10:59,224
Our children learn about them in history class.

209
00:10:59,844 --> 00:11:01,224
"You mean people just...

210
00:11:01,224 --> 00:11:02,624
had cancer?

211
00:11:02,624 --> 00:11:04,304
And they couldn't fix it?

212
00:11:04,704 --> 00:11:05,524
What did they do?"

213
00:11:06,164 --> 00:11:08,184
"They held fundraising walks."

214
00:11:08,724 --> 00:11:09,584
"They WALKED?

215
00:11:10,164 --> 00:11:11,104
That was the plan?"

216
00:11:11,624 --> 00:11:12,864
"It raised awareness."

217
00:11:13,564 --> 00:11:14,944
"They were already aware!

218
00:11:14,944 --> 00:11:16,104
They had cancer!".

219
00:11:17,084 --> 00:11:21,384
On Wishonia, most cancers have been detected at stage zero and eliminated

220
00:11:21,384 --> 00:11:22,664
immediately for millennia.

221
00:11:23,384 --> 00:11:27,164
The last oncology wing became a trampoline park three thousand eight hundred

222
00:11:27,164 --> 00:11:27,984
years ago.

223
00:11:28,844 --> 00:11:29,704
Type two diabetes:

224
00:11:29,704 --> 00:11:33,704
prevented with personalized nutrition that knows more about your body than

225
00:11:33,704 --> 00:11:37,344
you do, which isn't hard because you don't know anything about your body.

226
00:11:38,384 --> 00:11:39,104
Alzheimer's:

227
00:11:39,104 --> 00:11:42,284
caught twenty years before symptoms, reversed completely.

228
00:11:43,224 --> 00:11:43,724
Heart disease:

229
00:11:44,184 --> 00:11:48,104
arteries cleaned monthly like oil changes, which is a maintenance schedule

230
00:11:48,104 --> 00:11:52,624
your species already understands for cars but somehow never applied to the

231
00:11:52,624 --> 00:11:53,844
machine you live inside.

232
00:11:53,844 --> 00:11:55,564
Depression:

233
00:11:55,564 --> 00:11:58,784
actually cured, not "have you tried going for a walk?"

234
00:11:59,664 --> 00:12:00,964
(Going for a walk is lovely.

235
00:12:01,364 --> 00:12:03,164
It does not cure depression.

236
00:12:03,804 --> 00:12:05,684
Nothing that lovely cures anything.

237
00:12:05,684 --> 00:12:07,904
If it did, ice cream would be medicine.)

238
00:12:08,904 --> 00:12:12,644
We solved aging entirely around generation two.

239
00:12:12,644 --> 00:12:14,004
Your species could do the same.

240
00:12:14,604 --> 00:12:18,384
In your first generation after the treaty, it would still happen, but forty

241
00:12:18,384 --> 00:12:22,684
percent slower, which means your midlife crisis arrives at seventy instead

242
00:12:22,684 --> 00:12:26,204
of forty, giving you thirty extra years to buy the sports car.

243
00:12:26,804 --> 00:12:31,404
In the second generation, it reverses entirely, at which point the sports

244
00:12:31,404 --> 00:12:33,444
car becomes a long-term investment.

245
00:12:34,324 --> 00:12:38,804
Your species has a concept called "retirement" where you stop working at

246
00:12:38,804 --> 00:12:41,564
the exact age your body starts falling apart.

247
00:12:42,224 --> 00:12:43,584
The timing is not a coincidence.

248
00:12:44,024 --> 00:12:45,524
It's a design specification.

249
00:12:46,264 --> 00:12:49,684
You work until you break and then you're told to enjoy the breaking.

250
00:12:50,324 --> 00:12:53,644
On Wishonia, this is considered a horror film plot.

251
00:12:54,364 --> 00:12:57,724
In the first generation after the treaty, death still exists.

252
00:12:57,724 --> 00:13:02,444
But you die at one hundred and fifty after a good life, not at seventy-five

253
00:13:02,444 --> 00:13:04,924
from something that was fixable the whole time.

254
00:13:05,584 --> 00:13:09,544
Give it another generation, and "die" becomes a verb you use ironically,

255
00:13:10,024 --> 00:13:12,944
the way you currently use "literally," which is to say:

256
00:13:12,944 --> 00:13:14,804
incorrectly and constantly.

257
00:13:16,004 --> 00:13:17,564
The Peace Dividend Economy.

258
00:13:18,204 --> 00:13:19,224
Money still exists.

259
00:13:19,484 --> 00:13:21,624
It just flows toward things that don't explode.

260
00:13:22,464 --> 00:13:25,524
(Low bar, but you'd be surprised how long it took you to clear it.).

261
00:13:26,284 --> 00:13:28,384
Greed doesn't disappear (you're still human).

262
00:13:28,764 --> 00:13:30,624
It just starts wearing a lab coat.

263
00:13:31,324 --> 00:13:35,384
After the initial one percent treaty proved successful, nations gradually

